"In the surah, which begins with the glad tidings that the believers will attain salvation, the word falah (salvation) is explained. Falah means attaining one's desire and everlasting good. The first characteristic is faith. The second characteristic is being in a state of khushu (reverence and humility) in prayer. Khushu is feeling deep reverence in the heart before the majesty of Allah, and a state of tranquility in the body. With the revelation of this verse, the Companions cast their eyes down in prayer and attached great importance to khushu.\n\nThe third characteristic is turning away from vain and useless speech and deeds. The fourth is paying the zakah. The fifth is guarding one's chastity, abstaining from unlawful relations outside of spouses and concubines. The sixth is being faithful to trusts and covenants. The seventh is observing the prayers, that is, performing them on time and in accordance with their proper conditions. The believers who possess these qualities will inherit the Firdaus paradise. Firdaus is the highest of paradises.\n\nThen Allah demonstrates His power by recalling the stages of human creation. Man was first created from an extract (sulalah) drawn out of clay. Then this extract was made into a drop of fluid (nutfah) in a secure dwelling—the womb—followed by a clinging clot (alaqah), a lump of flesh (mudghah), bones, and the clothing of these bones with flesh, and then he was brought forth as an entirely new creation. This shows that Allah is the best of creators.\n\nThe word sulalah means to draw out or extract from something. In the creation of man, this expresses a stage preceding the first human, Adam. Some scholars have interpreted sulalah as foodstuffs. In any case, man was created from clay stage by stage. This creation is not the work of nature, but of a conscious creator. Indeed, death and resurrection are also requirements of this divine power.\n\nAllah has created seven paths (seven heavens) above us. These paths have been interpreted as the ascending pathways of angels or as seven systems. Allah is not unaware of creation; He knows all needs and creates everything according to a measure. He sends down rain from the sky in due measure, and with it He grows date-palm groves, vineyards, and olive trees. Benefits such as milk and meat are obtained from livestock. Cargo is also transported by ships. All of these are blessings of Allah.\n\nAlmighty Allah states that He sends down rain in due measure and settles it in the earth. This shows that He is not unaware of His creation. Rain is not like water distilled in a laboratory; within it lies divine will and artistry. For this reason, one should not deny the prayer for rain nor be astonished when such prayers are answered. Allah has sent water as a blessing; had He willed, He has the power to take it away. The drying up of waters during times of drought is a reminder of this.\n\nThrough water, Allah has grown for you gardens such as date-palm groves and vineyards. In these gardens, there are many fruits and benefits. He also created the olive tree originating from Mount Sinai (Tur Sina), which yields both oil and a condiment for food. By pointing to the blessed mountain where Moses (Musa) communed with Allah, this draws attention to the blessing of the olive. There are also lessons in livestock; the emergence of pure milk from between blood and dung is a proof of divine power. From them you derive benefits such as meat and transport.\n\nThe Prophet Noah (Nuh) told his people to worship Allah and to recognize no deity other than Him. However, the notables of his people saw him merely as a human being and rejected his prophethood. They said, \"Had Allah willed, He would have sent down angels,\" and accused him of madness. Noah implored his Lord, asking for help. Allah then revealed to him to build an ark and take the believers aboard. In the end, the disbelievers were drowned in the deluge.\n\nAfter the people of Noah, a new generation was brought forth. A prophet was sent to this generation as well. Yet once again the disbelievers denied prophethood, saying, \"He is only a human being like you; he eats of what you eat, and drinks of what you drink.\" They denied the Hereafter and claimed that the life of this world was the only life. The prophet sought refuge in Allah, and they were destroyed by a terrible blast. This also resembles the discourse of modern disbelievers.\n\nThen Moses and his brother Aaron were sent to Pharaoh and his chiefs. Pharaoh grew arrogant and rejected them. In the end, they were destroyed. Allah gave Moses the Scripture. Jesus, son of Mary, and his mother were also made a sign, and He sheltered them on a high ground with running water. Later, addressing the messengers, it was commanded that they eat of pure and lawful things and perform righteous deeds. The community of all the prophets is a single community, and Allah is their Lord.\n\nPeople divided their religion into fragments, each group rejoicing in what they had. But true believers tremble with reverence before their Lord, believe in His verses, associate no partners with Him, and perform good deeds while their hearts tremble with awe. They hasten to good deeds. Allah does not burden any soul beyond its capacity. In His presence is a record that speaks the truth.\n\nThe hearts of the disbelievers are in heedlessness. When they are seized with affliction while living in luxury, they cry out, but at that moment they will not be helped. For when the verses were recited to them, they would turn away and act arrogantly. They did not ponder this Quran; they did not recognize their Messenger. If the truth had conformed to their desires, the heavens and the earth would have been corrupted. They turned their backs on the Quran, which brought them their honor.\n\nAllah is the One Who created hearing, sight, and hearts. It is He Who created you on the earth and to Whose presence you will be gathered. It is He Who gives life and causes death, and to Him belongs the alternation of the night and the day. In spite of this, the deniers say, \"Resurrection after death is nothing but tales of the ancients.\" Ask them to whom the earth belongs, and they will say, \"To Allah.\" Yet they still do not reflect. Allah has not taken a son, nor is there any partner with Him. The sovereignty over all things belongs to Him.\n\nIn this section, Elmalılı notes that the deniers characterize religion as \"asatir\"—that is, fables of the ancients. Yet Islam is far removed from unfounded stories. The Quran refutes this claim by recalling the creation of the eyes, ears, and hearts.\n\nTo prove the oneness of Allah, the argument of \"temanu'\" (mutual prevention) is presented: If there were more than one deity, each would have governed what it created, and inevitably one would have overcome the other. This argument also appears in verse 22 of Surah al-Anbiya.\n\nThe Prophet is reassured that he will surely be shown the threat directed at the polytheists (worldly distress and the punishment of the Hereafter). Furthermore, it is commanded that evil be repelled in the best manner, and that refuge be sought in Allah from the incitements of devils and from their presence.\n\nIt is recounted that at the moment of death, the polytheists plead, \"My Lord, send me back to the world so that I may do good deeds.\" But this statement is futile; a barzakh (intermediate realm) awaits them, lasting until the Day of Resurrection. When the Trumpet (Sur) is blown, there will be no claims of lineage between them, nor will they ask after one another.\n\nIn the scale (mizan) where deeds are weighed, whoever's scales are heavy will attain salvation, while those whose scales are light will have ruined themselves and will abide in Hell forever. The Fire will scorch their faces. Allah reminds them that His verses were recited to them, but they rejected them. The polytheists confess that their transgression overcame them and beg to be brought out; however, Allah condemns them to remain abased and silent.\n\nThe believers' prayer, \"Our Lord! We have believed, so forgive us and have mercy upon us,\" is recalled. Because the polytheists mocked them, they forgot the remembrance of Allah. Today, Allah has rewarded the patient believers and truly granted them their desires. When the deniers are asked how many years they stayed on earth, they say, \"A day or part of a day\"; Allah, however, tells them that they stayed only a short while.\n\nAllah declares that He did not create humanity in vain and that they will return to His presence. Allah is Exalted; there is no deity except Him, the Lord of the Throne. Whoever worships another deity alongside Allah—for which he has no proof—his reckoning is with his Lord; the disbelievers will not attain salvation. At the end of the surah, the Prophet is commanded to say, \"My Lord, forgive and have mercy, for You are the best of the merciful.\""